The graphics and audio stack — Linux and Windows differ here
------------------------------------------------------------

On Linux these are system shared libraries: present on the machine, linked at run
time, and NOT redistributed by this project. They are Cairo (LGPL 2.1 / MPL 1.1),
FreeType (FTL / GPLv2 dual licence, used under the FTL), fontconfig (MIT-style),
libX11 (MIT), JACK's client library (LGPL 2.1), and the LV2 stack named above
(ISC).

On Windows there is no system copy of any of them, so the Windows release
STATICALLY LINKS and therefore redistributes them. That changes the licence
character of the shipped binary, so they are named here in full, with the versions
scripts/build-win-deps.sh pins:

  cairo 1.18.4       https://cairographics.org/
                     LGPL 2.1-or-later OR MPL 1.1 (dual).
  pixman 0.44.0      https://cairographics.org/     MIT.
  FreeType 2.13.3    https://freetype.org/          FTL OR GPLv2 (dual).
  libpng 1.6.48      http://libpng.org/pub/png/     libpng/zlib licence.
  zlib 1.3.1         https://zlib.net/              zlib licence.

  The LV2 stack is NOT part of the Windows build. Windows hosts VST3 only, so
  lilv, suil, serd, sord, sratom and zix are absent from that build entirely —
  see the LV2 build switch in CMakeLists.txt. Nor is JACK: Windows audio is ASIO
  with WASAPI as the fallback, and WinMM for MIDI. WASAPI and WinMM are part of
  the operating system; ASIO is a Steinberg SDK and has its own section below.

  fontconfig is NOT part of the Windows build either. It is linked on Linux but
  never called — there is no Fc* call anywhere in this tree — and FreeType opens
  the bundled .ttf files by path, so the Windows build simply drops it.

  FreeType: the FTL is elected, not the GPLv2 option. The FTL requires this credit
  to appear in the documentation of any product using it, so here it is verbatim:

      Portions of this software are copyright (c) 2024 The FreeType Project
      (www.freetype.org).  All rights reserved.

  FreeType is built with one modification, made by scripts/build-win-deps.sh
  rather than carried as a patch: its meson.build defines DLL_EXPORT on Windows
  without testing default_library, which makes a STATIC build export the whole
  FreeType API from whatever links it. The build script removes that define. No
  FreeType source file is changed.

  cairo and the LGPL: cairo is linked statically into the Windows binary, which
  LGPL 2.1 section 6 permits provided the recipient can relink the work against a
  modified cairo. That condition is met by publication rather than by a written
  offer: this project's complete corresponding source is published, the exact
  cairo version and the exact configuration used to build it are in
  scripts/build-win-deps.sh in this same repository, and the whole program
  rebuilds from that source with one cmake invocation. Anyone may substitute their
  own cairo and rebuild. No cairo source file is modified.

  WHICH OF CAIRO'S TWO ARMS IS ELECTED DEPENDS ON THE BUILD, and for the ASIO
  build it is not a free choice. cairo offers LGPL 2.1-or-later OR MPL 1.1.

    * The Windows ASIO build is GPLv3, so it ELECTS LGPL 2.1. MPL 1.1 is not
      GPL-compatible and would make that binary undistributable; LGPL 2.1
      section 3 expressly permits applying the ordinary GPL to a copy of the
      library, and says that where a version later than 2 has appeared "you can
      specify that version instead", which is what reaches GPLv3.
    * Every other build — Linux, the plug-ins, a WASAPI-only Windows build — is
      MIT, and either of cairo's arms serves it.

  So the MPL arm is still available to this project; it is simply not available
  to the one binary that carries ASIO. A future change that moved the ASIO build
  onto MPL 1.1 would be a licence breach rather than a preference.

